# Documentation architecture

## Layers (dependency order, abstract → concrete)

| Layer | Artifact type | Question answered |
|-------|---------------|-------------------|
| L1 | use_case      | For what end? |
| L2 | scenario      | Under what conditions, what happens? |
| L3 | business_rule | What invariant must always hold? |
| L4 | entity        | What data is involved? |
| L4 | attribute     | What field carries the value? |

## Dependency graph (machine-readable)

use_case
  └─ realized_by → scenario (1..n)
       └─ exercises → business_rule (1..n)
            └─ constrains → attribute (1..n)
                 └─ belongs_to → entity (1)

## Two directions of traversal

### Writing direction (top → bottom)
Order in which artifacts are CREATED. Each artifact requires its parent to exist.

1. use_case is written first (from business interview).
2. scenario is written to make the use_case testable.
3. business_rule is extracted when a scenario reveals an invariant.
4. attribute is added when a rule or scenario references a value.
5. entity is created to host attributes.

RULE: an agent MUST NOT create an artifact at layer N without
referencing at least one parent at layer N-1.

### Validation direction (bottom → top)
Order in which IMPACT is propagated when something changes.

1. attribute changes → find all related business_rules
2. business_rule changes → find all related scenarios
3. scenario changes → find all related use_cases
4. use_case changes → notify stakeholders

RULE: every change at any layer MUST trigger a validation pass
on all artifacts above it in the graph.

## Cross-cutting concerns

Business rules MAY reference attributes from multiple entities.
These are called "transverse rules" and live in:
  docs/02-behavior/business-rules/
NOT inside a single domain folder.

They are linked bidirectionally:
- The rule lists `related_entities` and `related_attributes` in frontmatter.
- Each entity lists `governed_by` in its own frontmatter (auto-generated
  by the pipeline from the inverse lookup).
